{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"dependent_variable":"classification accuracy (e.g., F1-score or accuracy on a held-out test set)","evidence_ids":["E2"],"expected_direction":"increase","gap_ids":["G1","G2"],"id":"H1","independent_variable":"feature standardisation (i.e., scaling features to unit variance)","rationale":"This hypothesis directly addresses the gap in evidence regarding the impact of feature scaling on KNN classification in tabular data. Although E2 discusses KNN performance in stereo vision, it does not test or evaluate feature scaling in tabular data. The hypothesis is falsifiable because if a tabular dataset with features on different scales shows no improvement or a decrease in classification accuracy after standardisation, the hypothesis is refuted. The expected direction (increase) is based on the general understanding in machine learning that feature scaling prevents features with large ranges from dominating distance calculations in KNN, thereby improving the geometric representation of data in feature space.","statement":"Standardising features prior to k-nearest-neighbour classification improves classification accuracy on tabular datasets with features on different scales."},"hash":"e23bad51557315544ff0b085284c14d33c149dd7d00893c4a4805228b6fe90f1","kind":"artefact","prev":"ff48e49ad48a6ded8610ce88ae22e2e788de8981f36e1e498bc72f972701b016","seq":22,"stage":"hypotheses","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:07.213+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"dependent_variable":"feature influence on distance metrics (e.g., mean absolute difference in feature contributions to Euclidean distance)","evidence_ids":["E2"],"expected_direction":"decrease","gap_ids":["G1","G2"],"id":"H2","independent_variable":"feature scaling (standardisation)","rationale":"This hypothesis focuses on a mechanistic aspect of KNN performance—how feature scaling affects the relative contribution of each feature to distance computation. While E2 does not evaluate this, it establishes that KNN performance in a spatial context is sensitive to feature representation. This hypothesis can be tested by computing the contribution of each feature to pairwise distances before and after standardisation in a public tabular dataset (e.g., from UCI or Kaggle). If the influence of high-magnitude features decreases after scaling, the hypothesis is supported; otherwise, it is falsified.","statement":"Feature scaling prior to k-nearest-neighbour classification reduces the influence of features with high magnitude on distance calculations, leading to more balanced neighborhood definitions in high-dimensional tabular data."},"hash":"e690fc69a5e3e75228147a27e133168f6ddadc6607fb5338d401f52e51e01f08","kind":"artefact","prev":"e23bad51557315544ff0b085284c14d33c149dd7d00893c4a4805228b6fe90f1","seq":23,"stage":"hypotheses","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:07.213+00:00"}
