{"actor":"director","data":{"action":"start","to":"experiment"},"hash":"64b42aaca390d76d7631714f6be892fb3fc5f5eb79c64b5080001f27a0e4ac29","kind":"stage","prev":"5beaeb3d61dab42f5481a65e6867e6d3448b657c8516663b9fe705339bcf1c88","seq":25,"stage":"design","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:07.213+00:00"}
{"actor":"experiment","data":{"control":"knn","datasets":["breast_cancer","wine_binary","digits_binary"],"folds":5,"hypothesis_id":"H1","id":"D1","metrics":["f1"],"model":"knn","rationale":"The experiment tests the hypothesis that standardising features improves classification accuracy in KNN on tabular datasets with features on different scales. The control condition (knn) evaluates performance without any feature scaling, while the treatment condition (knn+standardize) applies feature standardisation. The F1-score is used as the dependent variable because it directly measures classification performance in imbalanced or multi-class settings, which are common in tabular data. The three datasets represent diverse feature dimensions and sample sizes: breast_cancer (30 features, 569 samples), wine_binary (13 features, 178 samples), and digits_binary (64 features, 1797 samples), ensuring a range of scale and complexity. A paired Wilcoxon test is used because it assesses differences in F1 scores between the control and treatment conditions, accounting for the same model and dataset instances. Five-fold cross-validation ensures robust performance estimation, and three random seeds ensure reproducibility and reduce variance due to stochasticity. The choice of these datasets and conditions directly addresses the gap (G1, G2) by testing feature scaling in real tabular data, unlike E2 which focuses on stereo vision.","seeds":[123,456,789],"split":"stratified_kfold","test":"paired_wilcoxon","treatments":["knn+standardize"]},"hash":"76e9d1888522e1cd84e0b3e1b4044607bef81573e39c88590554cd6e6af15df9","kind":"artefact","prev":"64b42aaca390d76d7631714f6be892fb3fc5f5eb79c64b5080001f27a0e4ac29","seq":26,"stage":"design","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:07.213+00:00"}
{"actor":"experiment","data":{"control":"knn","datasets":["digits_binary"],"folds":5,"hypothesis_id":"H2","id":"D2","metrics":["mean_absolute_difference_in_feature_contributions_to_euclidean_distance"],"model":"knn","rationale":"The hypothesis concerns the influence of feature magnitude on distance metrics in KNN, specifically how feature scaling (standardisation) affects the relative contribution of each feature to Euclidean distance. To test this, we select 'digits_binary' (1797 x 64) as the dataset because it has high dimensionality and a large number of features, which amplifies the potential for high-magnitude features to dominate distance calculations. The control condition uses unstandardised KNN, while the treatment condition applies standardisation to the features before distance computation. We compute the mean absolute difference in feature contributions to Euclidean distance across all pairs of samples, both before and after standardisation. This metric directly reflects the hypothesis’s dependent variable. A paired Wilcoxon test is used to compare the distributions of these differences, as it is appropriate for non-normal, paired observations. Five-fold cross-validation ensures robustness, and two random seeds (123, 456) are used to ensure reproducibility and reduce variance due to randomness in data splits. The choice of 'digits_binary' ensures a high-dimensional, tabular context where feature scaling effects are most pronounced.","seeds":[123,456],"split":"stratified_kfold","test":"paired_wilcoxon","treatments":["knn+standardize"]},"hash":"7acdef8f301f06d7da9b70e839fe5e084823f3f6fb02ba2752f33cfbeb3a6e94","kind":"artefact","prev":"76e9d1888522e1cd84e0b3e1b4044607bef81573e39c88590554cd6e6af15df9","seq":27,"stage":"design","ts":"2026-10-10T06:03:07.213+00:00"}
